WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:11.180
There’s no better way to describe them;
The Netherlands is one of the lowest-lying

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:12.620
countries in the world.

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:24.320
And believe me when I say, these are lowlands.

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:28.330
The average elevation is only 30 meters and
about a quarter of Dutch territory is below

00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:31.440
sea level.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:35.260
This also makes the Netherlands one of the
countries most under threat from possible

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:38.050
rising sea levels caused by global warming.

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:42.690
But wait, it’s not the most threatened country.

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:46.840
As many of you will know, the Maldives is
most at risk with an average altitude of just

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:47.840
two meters.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:52.020
Which is incredible, especially if we consider
that despite being a flat island in the middle

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:56.780
of the ocean, its capital, Malé, is one of
the most densely populated cities on the entire

00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:00.300
planet.

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:03.850
Having said that, we’ll leave the Maldives
for another occasion because in this video

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:08.460
we’ll focus on the Netherlands and some
of the most ominous predictions that it currently

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:11.680
faces.

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:14.910
(CHART)

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:17.580
You may have seen images like the one appearing
now on your screen.

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:22.720
Images, models and maps showing how most of
the territory of the Netherlands – including

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:28.020
its capital, Amsterdam, will disappear under
the sea as a result of the melting of the

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:30.690
polar ice caps.

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:34.670
Logically it would follow that such an event
would eventually force the Dutch to emigrate

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:37.420
en masse to neighbouring countries like Germany.

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:42.150
Because according to scientific estimates,
the sea level could rise between 30 to 77

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:45.690
centimeters by the year 2100.

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:50.869
The worst case scenarios predict rises of
200 centimeters, two meters.

00:01:50.869 --> 00:01:56.080
And, even though that may not sound very much,
those centimetres really count.

00:01:56.080 --> 00:02:00.390
Not only because a rise in the sea level would
flood many lands as low-lying as the Netherlands,

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:05.390
but also because it would multiply the flood
damage, for example causing floods and river

00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:07.070
overflows when storms occur.

00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:11.340
This, of course, would translate into a loss
of human lives, destroyed crops and cities,

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:15.780
flooded land, etc., etc.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:20.920
However, just a second, because in this video
we’re going to show you how the inhabitants

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:25.170
of the Netherlands are already working to
face this gigantic challenge: dealing with

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:26.170
the rising sea level.

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:28.730
It’s a problem for which they only really
have two options.

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:29.730
No more, no less.

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:33.220
The first is to fight the water, building
higher levees, larger and more resistant dams

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:39.510
and more powerful containment infrastructure.

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:44.010
The second option is to embrace their neighbor,
water, and try to control and live with it.

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:47.240
And we should note, we’re talking about
a country with a lot of experience in the

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:48.920
matter.

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:52.340
People living on the land that we currently
know as The Netherlands have been suffering

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:57.220
the effects of huge floods that periodically
ended the lives of dozens to tens of thousands

00:02:57.220 --> 00:03:01.290
of people for centuries, even before the Middle
Ages.

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:04.900
Take the so-called Santa Lucia flood of 1287
that took the lives of between 50,000 and

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:05.900
80,000 people.

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:06.980
And you know what?

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:10.430
Even back then they already had containment
dams, a hydraulic achievement that was quite

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:11.490
advanced for the time.

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:14.550
We’re talking more than 700 years ago.

00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:20.010
So, as you can see, this is a population that
is very used to living with and fighting against

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:21.010
water.

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:22.430
But now the big questions that crop up are...

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:24.090
How on earth are the Dutch preparing for the
future?

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:26.190
Are they fighting a losing battle?

00:03:26.190 --> 00:03:30.460
Can the Netherlands even survive global warming
if the poles partially melt and raise the

00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:32.860
sea level?

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:36.900
Listen up.

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:42.370
(NATURAL RISK OR HUMAN RISK?)

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:43.520
So it’s no surprise.

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:49.990
Obviously, rising sea levels are a great risk
for the Netherlands and much of the world.

00:03:49.990 --> 00:03:54.580
And it’s true that if there’s one thing
that characterizes human beings, it’s their

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:59.740
ability to adapt, face adversity and overcome
virtually any risk that nature throws at them.

00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:01.990
Right now, this threat is on the table.

00:04:01.990 --> 00:04:02.990
So…

00:04:02.990 --> 00:04:04.720
What exactly is the risk we are talking about
right now?

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:09.360
Is the sea the risk or are we, humans, the
real risk?

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:15.720
Look, this map shows all the areas that the
Netherlands has reclaimed from the sea since

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:17.060
the fourteenth century.

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:21.440
As you can see, the Dutch have been swallowing
areas of the sea for centuries, building cities

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:25.950
and planting crops in areas with a high risk
of flooding, areas that were once under water

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:30.870
and could well be again one day.

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:35.350
After all, the sea has been redrawing the
map for millions and millions of years.

00:04:35.350 --> 00:04:40.020
The waters have advanced and receded, until
we got more or less the map that we know today,

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:42.350
which was completed about 6,000 years ago.

00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:46.080
In geological time, 6,000 years was practically
the day before yesterday.

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:50.250
However, now, the problem emerges because
the pace of natural warming has accelerated

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:51.250
due to human activity.

00:04:51.250 --> 00:04:52.330
That’s precisely what seems to be happening.

00:04:52.330 --> 00:04:57.790
In order to understand the worldwide situation
and specifically that of the Netherlands better,

00:04:57.790 --> 00:05:02.840
we can say that we’re facing two major challenges,
and the truth is that both are fundamentally

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:06.750
man-made.

00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:11.060
The first challenge is to slow global warming
to levels as close as possible to natural

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:12.060
warming.

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:16.550
And the second, to avoid occupying high-risk
spaces such as polders; which are lands reclaimed

00:05:16.550 --> 00:05:23.820
from the sea, or flood plains near rivers,
which are areas susceptible to natural floods.

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:27.750
Let me ask you a question, if I build my house
in the flood zone next to a river and, one

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:32.000
day, a flood comes and it destroys my house,
is it my fault or is it the fault of the river

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:33.770
and the flood?

00:05:33.770 --> 00:05:38.790
Perhaps, from a general point of view, many
of us would blame the river, but in the Netherlands

00:05:38.790 --> 00:05:41.980
they have a deep-rooted culture of coexistence
with water.

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:44.300
To some extent, they know that they are the
guests.

00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:48.949
That is, that they occupy a place that the
sea used to occupy and in which they need

00:05:48.949 --> 00:05:52.360
to continue coexisting.

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:53.960
And that’s what makes the Dutch different,
unique but...

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:55.419
Do you want some concrete examples?

00:05:55.419 --> 00:05:56.419
Well...

00:05:56.419 --> 00:06:00.760
Let’s take a look.

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:07.120
(THE BATTLE AGAINST THE SEA, STRATEGY CHANGE?)

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:10.770
If the inhabitants of these lowlands have
learned anything over the years, it’s that

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:13.180
fighting against the sea is a losing battle.

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:14.950
A certain failure.

00:06:14.950 --> 00:06:17.400
Let me explain.

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:21.580
The strategy of protecting themselves through
dikes, dune barriers, dams and reservoirs

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:24.710
to control rising sea levels has been used
for centuries, particularly during the twentieth

00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:25.710
century.

00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:28.480
The greatest example of this public strategy
of protection against the sea was the Delta

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:33.530
Works project, developed after the great flood
of 1953 that killed almost 2,000 people in

00:06:33.530 --> 00:06:38.430
a single night.

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:42.060
The project was completed in 1997, with the
construction of Maeslantkering, a gigantic

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:46.900
barrier near Rotterdam with twin gates as
large as two Eiffel towers that rotate on

00:06:46.900 --> 00:06:48.460
tracks to seal the waterway.

00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:49.979
They take two hours to close.

00:06:49.979 --> 00:06:53.460
Over the past two decades, the Maeslantkering
barrier gates have not yet been used to contain

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:56.870
any massive flooding, but the Netherlands
pushed the Delta Plan forward with the aim

00:06:56.870 --> 00:07:02.729
of creating effective protection for at least
200 years.

00:07:02.729 --> 00:07:04.960
But hold on a minute, because we’re overlooking
something.

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:08.870
The Delta Plan is a project from the last
century and the truth is that over the last

00:07:08.870 --> 00:07:14.830
decades knowledge about rising sea levels
and their effects has increased a lot.

00:07:14.830 --> 00:07:18.580
That’s exactly why the Netherlands has now
changed its perspective.

00:07:18.580 --> 00:07:22.720
You see, after Delta Works, the Dutch have
switched from planning for more and more and

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:24.860
higher levees, to planning for controlled
floods.

00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:26.450
Yes, you heard that right.

00:07:26.450 --> 00:07:27.450
Controlled flooding.

00:07:27.450 --> 00:07:31.919
In fact, very recently, in March 2019, they
finished construction on the Room for the

00:07:31.919 --> 00:07:38.580
River project, a huge project with a budget
of about 3 billion dollars.

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:42.389
A project that has sought to expand river
floodplains where there’s a high overflow

00:07:42.389 --> 00:07:43.389
risk.

00:07:43.389 --> 00:07:45.699
Basically, it involves diverting flood waters
to controlled areas.

00:07:45.699 --> 00:07:46.699
Sounds sensible, right?

00:07:46.699 --> 00:07:47.699
(AUDIO: "If the water comes in, from the rivers
or the sea, we can evacuate maybe 15 out of

00:07:47.699 --> 00:07:48.699
100 people.

00:07:48.699 --> 00:07:49.699
So evacuation isn’t an option.

00:07:49.699 --> 00:07:50.699
We can escape only into high buildings.

00:07:50.699 --> 00:07:51.699
We have no choice.

00:07:51.699 --> 00:07:52.699
We must learn to live with water.”

00:07:52.699 --> 00:07:53.699
- Ahmed Aboutaleb, Mayor of Rotterdam)

00:07:53.699 --> 00:07:55.210
Well, one of the best examples we can find
of this plan is Eendragtspolder, a fundamentally

00:07:55.210 --> 00:07:59.650
agricultural polder in which they’ve created
a large aquatic recreation area.

00:07:59.650 --> 00:08:03.789
However, as you can imagine, leisure isn’t
the main objective of this huge body of water

00:08:03.789 --> 00:08:08.449
and canals, rather it’s designed to absorb
and contain water during floods and large

00:08:08.449 --> 00:08:09.449
storms.

00:08:09.449 --> 00:08:12.870
It is close to Rotterdam and also to the lowest
point in the Netherlands, so it’s one of

00:08:12.870 --> 00:08:15.850
the areas most likely to flood in the entire
country.

00:08:15.850 --> 00:08:19.759
This kind of reservoir is designed to prevent
the spread of water during the overflows of

00:08:19.759 --> 00:08:24.000
the Rhine River, which is expected to happen
periodically, once every ten years.

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:28.780
But don’t think that’s the end of it,
because apart from the new polders that are

00:08:28.780 --> 00:08:37.740
capable of absorbing floods, the Dutch government’s
policies and those of its cities go much further.

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:41.670
Dutch engineers have been preparing the country
for decades, keeping a worst-case scenario

00:08:41.670 --> 00:08:43.300
in mind and always thinking long-term.

00:08:43.300 --> 00:08:46.149
We’re talking 100, 200 or even 1,000 years.

00:08:46.149 --> 00:08:50.339
That’s a great way to prepare.

00:08:50.339 --> 00:08:53.959
In Rotterdam, one of the cities with the highest
risk of flooding, they are creating a large

00:08:53.959 --> 00:08:58.259
number of public spaces to evacuate water
and thus help prevent a lot of damage.

00:08:58.259 --> 00:09:03.189
For example, there’s the creation of water
squares such as Benthemplein, an area that

00:09:03.189 --> 00:09:07.820
in case of floods or storms turns the lower
areas of the enclosure, which usually hold

00:09:07.820 --> 00:09:12.670
leisure and sports facilities, into gigantic
swimming pools that can hold up to 1.7 million

00:09:12.670 --> 00:09:13.670
liters of water.

00:09:13.670 --> 00:09:16.450
All that water that would’ve caused enormous
damage and losses to the surrounding areas,

00:09:16.450 --> 00:09:18.360
drains gradually to avoid mass flooding.

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:20.259
But wait, because there’s more.

00:09:20.259 --> 00:09:24.999
Garages, yes, yes, even garages are being
designed as water tanks in case of emergency.

00:09:24.999 --> 00:09:28.990
The fundamental idea in Rotterdam and other
vulnerable cities is to create as many controlled

00:09:28.990 --> 00:09:32.699
water tanks as possible to safeguard homes
and major infrastructure.

00:09:32.699 --> 00:09:34.869
That is, to allow flooding, but to control
it.

00:09:34.869 --> 00:09:39.380
And all this will greatly reduce economic
costs after a natural disaster or major weather

00:09:39.380 --> 00:09:41.279
event.

00:09:41.279 --> 00:09:46.939
And the best part is that both citizens and
politicians share this vision.

00:09:46.939 --> 00:09:52.259
(AUDIO: “We can’t just keep building higher
levees, because we will end up living behind

00:09:52.259 --> 00:09:53.279
10-meter walls.

00:09:53.279 --> 00:09:56.170
We need to give the rivers more places to
flow.

00:09:56.170 --> 00:10:00.050
Protection against climate change is only
as strong as the weakest link in the chain,

00:10:00.050 --> 00:10:04.121
and the chain in our case includes not just
the big gates and dams at the sea but a whole

00:10:04.121 --> 00:10:09.850
philosophy of spatial planning, crisis management,
children’s education, online apps and public

00:10:09.850 --> 00:10:10.850
spaces.”

00:10:10.850 --> 00:10:13.230
- Harold van Waveren, Dutch government advisor)

00:10:13.230 --> 00:10:14.230
And we are still not done.

00:10:14.230 --> 00:10:18.029
In what may be somewhat more controversial,
the government is expropriating agricultural

00:10:18.029 --> 00:10:20.470
land from farmers to create controlled flood
zones.

00:10:20.470 --> 00:10:23.989
They’re doing this without asking or searching
for volunteers.

00:10:23.989 --> 00:10:25.369
Farmers just have to leave.

00:10:25.369 --> 00:10:29.130
Safety above all, at least that's what they
say.

00:10:29.130 --> 00:10:33.959
However, many of these farmers are seeing
an opportunity to change, to create something

00:10:33.959 --> 00:10:37.480
new and to adapt to a future with more water
in the Netherlands.

00:10:37.480 --> 00:10:42.319
Ok, true, with the compensation money, some
farmers have chosen to retire, but there are

00:10:42.319 --> 00:10:44.939
other much more curious reinvention examples.

00:10:44.939 --> 00:10:47.389
Such as the invention of floating farms.

00:10:47.389 --> 00:10:48.529
Yes, you heard it right.

00:10:48.529 --> 00:10:49.529
Floating farms.

00:10:49.529 --> 00:10:50.529
Listen up.

00:10:50.529 --> 00:10:53.420
With financial support from the city of Rotterdam,
the Beladon company has created the first

00:10:53.420 --> 00:10:54.499
floating dairy farm.

00:10:54.499 --> 00:10:58.879
At the moment, it’s only a two million euro
prototype for 40 cows that could produce half

00:10:58.879 --> 00:11:00.730
a million liters of milk per year.

00:11:00.730 --> 00:11:04.399
And perhaps if the costs were lower, inventions
such as this would allow the Netherlands to

00:11:04.399 --> 00:11:08.820
alleviate some of the problems that the elimination
of agricultural land for the benefit of waterways

00:11:08.820 --> 00:11:10.230
can pose for its economy.

00:11:10.230 --> 00:11:14.189
They are, after all, the world's second largest
exporter of agricultural products, second

00:11:14.189 --> 00:11:20.769
only to the United States, with more than
90 billion euros in exports in 2018.

00:11:20.769 --> 00:11:23.249
As you may have gathered from this video,
the Dutch won’t give up easily.

00:11:23.249 --> 00:11:27.069
They have always lived side by side with water,
and it seems that they’ll continue to do

00:11:27.069 --> 00:11:28.350
so for generations.

00:11:28.350 --> 00:11:32.750
That’s why they’ve been adapting for the
worst-case scenarios for decades: a sea level

00:11:32.750 --> 00:11:37.579
rise of up to 77 centimeters or even more
by 2100.

00:11:37.579 --> 00:11:44.089
Nor should we be surprised by the huge capacity
for adaptation that human beings have shown

00:11:44.089 --> 00:11:46.399
when faced with all kinds of scenarios.

00:11:46.399 --> 00:11:49.689
The point is that it doesn’t look like the
Netherlands will disappear underwater.

00:11:49.689 --> 00:11:51.470
At least not in the coming decades, don't
you think?

00:11:51.470 --> 00:11:56.140
The apocalyptic scenarios will only occur
if the Dutch stand still for decades or centuries

00:11:56.140 --> 00:11:57.140
without acting.

00:11:57.140 --> 00:12:01.250
And today, here, on VisualPolitik, we’ve
shown you that that’s simply not the case.

00:12:01.250 --> 00:12:04.269
Obviously, this doesn’t mean that global
warming isn’t a challenge, a challenge that

00:12:04.269 --> 00:12:06.290
we all have to address and overcome.

00:12:06.290 --> 00:12:09.579
Like so many others.

00:12:09.579 --> 00:12:14.809
However, what we have wanted to show in this
video is how some societies have already begun

00:12:14.809 --> 00:12:19.089
to prepare for tomorrow's world and how perhaps
looking for solutions is better than getting

00:12:19.089 --> 00:12:21.060
carried away with sensationalism, don't you
think?

00:12:21.060 --> 00:12:22.110
But now it's your turn.

00:12:22.110 --> 00:12:25.120
The Netherlands will be with us for a while,
that seems clear.

00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:26.120
But...

00:12:26.120 --> 00:12:28.569
Do you think the time will come when not even
the most advanced technologies will allow

00:12:28.569 --> 00:12:30.199
the Dutch to live in their country?

00:12:30.199 --> 00:12:34.329
And in your country’s case, how do you think
government and society would respond to such

00:12:34.329 --> 00:12:35.329
a challenge?

00:12:35.329 --> 00:12:36.329
Leave your answer in the comments.

00:12:36.329 --> 00:12:40.089
So I really hope you enjoyed this video, please
hit like if you did, and don’t forget to

00:12:40.089 --> 00:12:41.600
subscribe for brand new videos.

